{#
  ArcOS OSPFv2 + OSPFv3 (issue #39). Ported from the out-of-tree netlab/arcos/ device
  (live-verified: full adjacency both directions over gNMI). Same area/area-interface shape
  for both AFs modulo two differences: the protocol keyword (OSPF vs OSPF3), and the area ID is
  written as a plain integer ("area 0") in OSPFv3, not dotted-decimal ("area 0.0.0.0") like
  OSPFv2 -- confirmed in the ArcOS 8.5 CLI docs.

  KNOWN OPEN ISSUE (found via tests/integration/ospf/ospfv2/01-network.yml, a generic
  cross-vendor test against FRR probe nodes -- arcos-arcos adjacencies are unaffected): the
  arrcus_arcos containerlab kind brings interfaces up with a much larger default veth MTU
  (8974) than the frr kind's (1500). OSPF's DBD exchange checks MTU compatibility, so an
  ArcOS<->FRR adjacency stalls indefinitely in NEIGHBOR_EXSTART. Explicitly setting ArcOS's own
  `interface <if> mtu <n>` config leaf to 1500 to match does NOT resolve it (tested live) --
  the OSPF process appears to check the underlying kernel veth MTU directly, not the
  configured-intent leaf. No `mtu-ignore`-equivalent OSPF leaf was found under `interface
  <if> subinterface 0` (the ArcOS 8.5 OpenConfig-CLI subinterface schema has no mtu child at
  all; `interface <if> mtu` is a top-level, not OSPF-specific, config leaf). Root cause is at
  the containerlab-kind level (arrcus_arcos's own default veth MTU), not something this
  template or netlab's clab.mtu device setting can reach -- clab.mtu is never actually
  propagated into the rendered containerlab topology by netsim/templates/provider/clab/clab.j2
  for ANY device (grepped: no `mtu` handling exists there at all), so it was never a viable
  lever here regardless. Follow-up: investigate whether the arrcus_arcos kind definition
  itself accepts an MTU override, or find the real ArcOS OSPF MTU-check bypass.
#}
{% macro area_interfaces(af) %}
{% for l in interfaces|default([]) if l.ospf is defined and l[af] is defined %}
  interface {{ l.ifname }}
{# BUG FIX (issue #39, found via a generic cross-vendor OSPF test against an FRR probe --
   tests/integration/ospf/ospfv2/01-network.yml, which explicitly sets ospf.network_type:
   broadcast on one link and point-to-point on another between the SAME two devices):
   1) netlab's canonical ospf.network_type value is "point-to-point" (hyphen) -- see
      netsim/modules/ospf.py's network_type() allowed-value list. This template (and the
      out-of-tree reference it was ported from) checked for "point_to_point" (underscore),
      which never matched.
   2) The `l.type == 'p2p'` fallback (inherited from the out-of-tree reference) unconditionally
      forced POINT_TO_POINT_NETWORK on any 2-node link regardless of an explicit
      ospf.network_type -- so even after fixing (1), a link explicitly marked
      `ospf.network_type: broadcast` still rendered as point-to-point (a 2-node link is always
      netlab type p2p), stalling the FRR<->ArcOS adjacency in ExStart when the two sides then
      disagreed on network type. Fix: only fall back to l.type == 'p2p' when
      ospf.network_type was NOT explicitly set; an explicit "broadcast" now renders nothing
      (ArcOS's own default network-type), matching netlab's semantics for every other device.
   The out-of-tree reference's own test topologies never explicitly set ospf.network_type at
   all, so neither bug was ever exercised there. #}
{% set nt = l.ospf.network_type|default('point-to-point' if l.type|default('') == 'p2p' else '') %}
{% if nt == 'point-to-point' %}
   network-type POINT_TO_POINT_NETWORK
{% endif %}
{% if l.ospf.cost is defined %}
   metric {{ l.ospf.cost }}
{% endif %}
{% if l.ospf.bfd|default(ospf.bfd|default(false)) %}
   bfd enabled true
{% endif %}
{% if l.ospf.priority is defined %}
   priority {{ l.ospf.priority }}
{% endif %}
{% if l.ospf.timers.hello is defined %}
   timers hello-interval {{ l.ospf.timers.hello }}
{% endif %}
{% if l.ospf.timers.dead is defined %}
   timers dead-interval {{ l.ospf.timers.dead }}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endmacro %}
{% set pid = ospf.process|default(1) %}
{% if ospf.router_id is defined and ospf.af.ipv4|default(true) %}
network-instance default protocol OSPF p{{ pid }}
 global router-id {{ ospf.router_id }}
 global log-adjacency-changes LOG_ADJ_ENABLE_DETAILED
 area {{ ospf.area | default("0.0.0.0") }}
{% if loopback.ipv4 is defined %}
  interface loopback0
{% endif %}
{{ area_interfaces('ipv4') }}
{% endif %}
{% if ospf.router_id is defined and ospf.af.ipv6|default(false) %}
{% set area3 = '0' if (ospf.area|default('0.0.0.0')) == '0.0.0.0' else ospf.area %}
network-instance default protocol OSPF3 p{{ pid }}
 global router-id {{ ospf.router_id }}
 global log-adjacency-changes LOG_ADJ_ENABLE_DETAILED
 area {{ area3 }}
{% if loopback.ipv6 is defined %}
  interface loopback0
{% endif %}
{{ area_interfaces('ipv6') }}
{% endif %}
